        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Silane and siloxane are two sizes of the same idea. Silane is small and goes deep, which suits dense concrete under salt and freeze-thaw. Siloxane is larger and stays nearer the surface, which suits porous brick, block and stucco. Blends combine both, which is why they dominate store shelves and suit most homes.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Choose by surface, check the active content on the data sheet, and remember what neither can do: stop oil, fill cracks or hold back water under pressure. Clean and dry the surface, apply at the full label rate in mild weather, and confirm with a water test.
          </p>
